    "body": "> What I want is `cmd+k` should work as same as `ctrl+l`.\r\n\r\nWhy should they? They are not the same thing and that's why they are different shortcuts.\r\n\r\nAgain, quoting from the link you provided:\r\n\r\n> One difference is that Cmd+K **clears the scrollback buffer** as well as the screen.\r\n\r\n\"Scrollback buffer\" here essentially includes your prompt, so the behavior is pretty much correct.\r\n\r\nAlso please don't just ignore my other questions. You still don't mention what terminal you are using, and `cmd+k` doesn't work in some terminals. Repeat, non-standards should not be given any special care, but pull requests to support it are welcome.\r\n\r\nYou should either just press `ctrl+l` or if you prefer `cmd+k` because it's easier to reach, you should just rebind it to `ctrl+l` in your terminal or use something like Karabiner-Elements.",
